// ============================================================
// Hour 15 — Schema Violation Alert (KQL Queryset scheduled alert)
// ============================================================
// WHAT: Scheduled KQL query every 5 min over SchemaViolationRate.
//       Returns a ROW only when violation_rate > 2% in the last 10 min
//       → Activator sends ONE email. Empty result when healthy → silence.
//
// WHY NOT Data Activator on Eventstream (per-event)?
//   Event arrives → null user_id? → email  × N malformed = N emails
//   Plus: Activator treats "" as a MISSING field → never sees real nulls.
//
// WHY THIS (aggregation-based, like PagerDuty / Datadog / Grafana / CloudWatch):
//   Every 5 min → "what's the rate?" → 0.45% = empty = no email
//                                     → 5.2%  = one row = ONE email
//   Care about the RATE, not any single malformed event.
//
// WHY 2% threshold?
//   Normal ≈ 0.45% (chaos 0.5% malformed). 2% ≈ 4× buffer above normal:
//     0.45% healthy → no alert
//     1.5% variance → no alert
//     2%+  abnormal → alert
//     10%+ catastrophe → alert
//   Too low (0.5%) = false alarms; too high (10%) = miss moderate degradation.
//
// WHY ago(10m) while scheduling every 5m? — intentional OVERLAP
//   Exact 5m lookback + scheduler 3s late = a 3s blind spot that can hide a spike.
//   10m lookback every 5m → 5m overlap → every moment covered by ≥1 run.
//   Checking twice is harmless (alert is on aggregated rate, not per event).
//   Analogy: cameras that each record 10 min, a new one starts every 5 min —
//   even if one starts late, no blind spot.
//
// WHY SchemaViolationRate (materialized view) not raw_events?
//   MV already has total + violations per 5-min bucket → query reads 2–3 rows,
//   not 1.14M. That's the Hour 13 payoff.
//
// SETUP (Fabric UI):
//   1. Open pipeline_health_queries KQL Queryset
//   2. Paste + Run (EMPTY when healthy / no recent live data — correct)
//   3. Toolbar → "Set alert" → Activator, every 5 min, Email
//      Fires only when the query returns a NON-EMPTY result
//   4. Test: python -m tests.test_trigger_kql_alert
//      (100 events over ~2 min, 60% real nulls — dominates the 5-min MV bucket)
//
// NOTE ON TESTING:
//   ago(10m) looks at clock-now. Historical July 1–14 sim data → empty.
//   The minimal 8-event script (test_trigger_schema_violation.py) is for
//   Eventstream Activator demos ONLY — 8 events get drowned by thousands of
//   other rows in the same 5-min MV bucket, so rate never clears 2%.
//   Use test_trigger_kql_alert.py (60% malformed burst) for this alert.
// ============================================================


// ---- Production alert query (schedule every 5 min) ----
SchemaViolationRate
| where ts > ago(10m)
| summarize
    total_events = sum(total),
    total_violations = sum(violations)
| extend violation_rate = todouble(total_violations) / todouble(total_events) * 100
| where violation_rate > 2.0
| project alert_message = strcat(
    "Schema violation rate spiked to ", tostring(round(violation_rate, 2)),
    "% in the last 10 minutes. Total violations: ", tostring(total_violations),
    " out of ", tostring(total_events), " events.")


// ---- Optional: dry-run against ALL historical MV buckets (no ago filter) ----
// Use this ONLY to sanity-check the aggregation math offline — do NOT schedule it.
